<h2>The sizing trap nobody warns a small bakery about</h2>
<p>Here is a problem almost every small bakery, café, and patisserie discovers the hard way, usually a few months after opening: <strong>there is no obvious machine to wash your baking trays.</strong></p>

<p>You have stacks of 600×400 mm trays coming out of the oven all day — sticky with caramelized sugar, slick with polymerized fat, crusted with baked-on egg wash. Your staff are washing them by hand in a sink that's too small, taking turns at the job nobody wants, for hours. So you go looking for a machine. And you walk straight into a trap with two jaws:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The commercial dishwasher you can afford and fit won't take the tray.</strong> Its rack is 500×500 mm. Your tray is 600×400 mm. It does not go in.</li>
  <li><strong>The tray washer that <em>can</em> take the tray won't fit your shop.</strong> It's a rack-conveyor or tunnel machine built for a wholesale plant — metres of floor, a drain pit, industrial three-phase power.</li>
</ul>

<p>Most operators bounce between these two dead ends for a while, then give up and keep hand-washing. That's the gap the V-TAI JD-3 was designed to close. Before the solution, it's worth understanding exactly why each of the two "obvious" machines fails — because the reason is more specific than most buyers realize.</p>

<h2>Why a standard commercial dishwasher physically can't do it</h2>
<p>This surprises people, so let's be precise: the limitation is <strong>geometry, not power.</strong> The global standard for a commercial dishwasher rack is 500×500 mm. That dimension wasn't chosen for bakeries — it was chosen for plates, glasses, cups, and saucers, which is what most commercial dishwashers were designed to wash.</p>

<p>A 600×400 mm tray — the <a href="/blog/600x400-tray-size-history-and-standard/" class="underline">European Bakery Norm (EN 631)</a> size that every modern combi oven and proofer is built around — is 100 mm longer on its long side than a 500×500 rack. It cannot lie flat in the basket. It cannot stand vertically without fouling the wash arms. Push the door down and it won't close. No amount of heat or pressure helps, because the tray never makes it into the chamber in the first place.</p>

<p>This is why bakery owners who buy a conventional under-counter or hood dishwasher — even a good one — end up using it for plates and cups while <em>still</em> hand-washing the trays. The machine was never sized for the one item the bakery has the most of.</p>

<h2>Why a tunnel / rack-conveyor washer is the wrong tool for a shop</h2>
<p>The other end of the trap is the industrial tray washer. These machines genuinely can clean trays — that's what they're for. But they are built for a wholesale bakery or central kitchen, and dropping one into a retail shop is like parking a delivery truck in a one-car garage. Consider what a rack-conveyor or flight-type tunnel washer actually demands:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Floor.</strong> A straight run of 3–6 metres for the load side, tunnel, and unload side. Most retail bakeries are 40–80 m² total — you don't have a spare 6-metre lane.</li>
  <li><strong>Drainage.</strong> A dedicated floor drain or drain pit, plumbed for continuous high-volume discharge.</li>
  <li><strong>Power.</strong> Three-phase service typically in the 18–40 kW range, far beyond a small shop's panel.</li>
  <li><strong>Ventilation.</strong> A make-up-air and exhaust hood to handle the continuous steam plume.</li>
  <li><strong>Volume.</strong> The economics only work if you're washing hundreds of trays an hour, every hour. A single-shop bakery never reaches that.</li>
</ul>

<p>So the tunnel washer isn't "too expensive" so much as <strong>architecturally impossible</strong> for the space — and wildly oversized for the actual job. You'd be buying a factory line to wash 150 trays a day.</p>

<h2>The gap — and the machine sized for it</h2>
<p>The JD-3 exists precisely in the space between those two dead ends. It is a <strong>hood-type</strong> machine — you slide a rack in, pull the hood down, and the cycle runs — but it is engineered around the 600×400 tray rather than around plates.</p>

<p>The number that matters most: the body is <strong>804 × 816 mm with the hood closed — a 0.65 m² footprint.</strong> That is the same floor area as a single under-counter dishwasher, or a small stainless prep table. You need 2,115 mm of vertical clearance for the hood to lift, and roughly 200 mm of space behind for the hoses. That's it. If there is room against a wall for a prep table, there is room for a JD-3.</p>

<p>Inside that compact body is a <strong>650×550 mm rack</strong> — sized so a 600×400 tray drops in vertically with clearance, six trays to a cycle. So you get the tray capability of a dedicated tray washer in the floor footprint of a dishwasher. That single combination — tray-sized chamber, dishwasher-sized footprint — is the entire reason the machine exists.</p>

