<h2>Small footprint is only half the problem — a tray needs real cleaning power</h2>
<p>Fitting in the space is necessary but not sufficient. The reason you can't just put a 600×400 tray through a gentle, low-temperature machine is that <strong>a baking tray is not a dinner plate.</strong> A plate carries fresh, soft food residue. A tray carries residue that has been through an oven — <a href="/blog/chemistry-of-washing-bakery-trays/" class="underline">caramelized sugar, polymerized fat, baked egg wash, fermented dough crumb</a> — each one chemically bonded to the steel by heat. Lifting that takes two things a consumer or low-temp machine doesn't have: <strong>heat and mechanical force.</strong></p>

<p>This is where the JD-3's compact body hides genuinely industrial cleaning hardware:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Dual rotating wash arms (upper and lower).</strong> 360° omnidirectional spray, with nozzle geometry aimed specifically at the long, flat surfaces of a tray — where coverage matters most and where a poorly-aimed dishwasher leaves streaks.</li>
  <li><strong>High-temperature wash and rinse.</strong> The wash tank holds 60–65°C to soften and emulsify baked-on fat; the final rinse reaches ≥82°C — hot enough to sanitize to the <strong>NSF/ANSI 3</strong> standard and to flash-dry the trays as they come out.</li>
  <li><strong>High-pressure delivery.</strong> The 0.75 kW wash pump drives the arms hard enough to physically blast loosened residue off the surface, rather than just rinsing it.</li>
  <li><strong>A 2-minute cycle on 2.0–2.5 L of water.</strong> Six trays clean and sanitized every two minutes — 180 trays an hour — using a fraction of the water hand-washing or a loose-fitting machine would.</li>
</ul>

<p>Heat plus pressure, aimed at the tray. That's the combination that actually removes baked-on residue, and it's why a correctly-built compact machine outperforms both a hand-wash sink and an ill-fitting general dishwasher on the one job that matters.</p>

